          "guid": "mt2j4hnv58zsopd4",
          "message": {
            "text": "The dropdown half of this guard must open a REAL tracked menu; the context-menu half can stay a bare div — and the asymmetry is deliberate, not an oversight.\n\ndismissTransientOverlays dismisses the two menus by different mechanisms:\n\n- Dropdowns → closeAllMenus() [0], which closes only the handles tracked in dropdown.ts's openDropdowns Set. It does NOT sweep the DOM by class. A hand-mounted .dropdown-menu div is untracked, so it would survive the sweep — which is exactly why the pre-fix test failed (expected 1 to be 0). But an untracked dropdown cannot occur in production (every real menu goes through createDropdown), so the test now opens a real, tracked menu.\n- Context menus → closeContextMenu() [1], which removes every .context-menu by class. The real context menu is itself just a .context-menu div appended to body with no handle registry, so a bare div is a faithful stand-in there.\n\nSo the implementation was correct all along; the test was fabricating a state (an untracked dropdown) that the app never produces.\n",
            "markdown": "The dropdown half of this guard must open a REAL tracked menu; the context-menu half can stay a bare div — and the asymmetry is deliberate, not an oversight.\n\n`dismissTransientOverlays` dismisses the two menus by different mechanisms:\n\n- Dropdowns → `closeAllMenus()` [0], which closes only the handles tracked in `dropdown.ts`'s `openDropdowns` Set. It does NOT sweep the DOM by class. A hand-mounted `.dropdown-menu` div is untracked, so it would survive the sweep — which is exactly why the pre-fix test failed (`expected 1 to be 0`). But an untracked dropdown cannot occur in production (every real menu goes through `createDropdown`), so the test now opens a real, tracked menu.\n- Context menus → `closeContextMenu()` [1], which removes every `.context-menu` by class. The real context menu is itself just a `.context-menu` div appended to body with no handle registry, so a bare div is a faithful stand-in there.\n\nSo the implementation was correct all along; the test was fabricating a state (an untracked dropdown) that the app never produces.\n"
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "src/client/transientOverlays.test.ts"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 63,
                  "endLine": 82,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "\n  it('removes dropdown + context menus (a keyboard switch fires no outside click)', () => {\n    // HS-9706 — the dropdown must be a REAL one (`createDropdown`), not a bare\n    // `.dropdown-menu` div. `dismissTransientOverlays` closes dropdowns via\n    // `closeAllMenus()`, which closes only the handles tracked in `dropdown.ts`'s\n    // `openDropdowns` Set — it does NOT DOM-sweep by class. A hand-mounted div is\n    // untracked, so it would survive; but an untracked dropdown can't occur in\n    // production (every real menu goes through `createDropdown`), so the faithful\n    // guard opens a real, tracked menu. The context menu, by contrast, IS just a\n    // `.context-menu` div appended to body (no handle registry — `closeContextMenu`\n    // removes it by class), so a bare div is a faithful stand-in there.\n    const anchorEl = anchor();\n    createDropdown(anchorEl, [{ label: 'Item', key: 'i', action: () => { /* no-op */ } }]);\n    mountOverlay('context-menu');\n    expect(document.querySelectorAll('.dropdown-menu').length).toBe(1);\n\n    dismissTransientOverlays();\n    expect(document.querySelectorAll('.dropdown-menu').length).toBe(0);\n    expect(document.querySelectorAll('.context-menu').length).toBe(0);\n  });"
                  }
                }
              }
            }
          ],
